    TXT,
    'article' => [
        'intro' => <<<'TXT'
        Ohio sales tax works out Sales tax from Purchase amount and ZIP code. It is a single relationship that reads sales tax out of purchase amount, not a table of canned results keyed by them.
        TXT,
        'sections' => [
            ['title' => 'How the Ohio sales tax calculator works', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship behind Ohio sales tax: it takes 2 numbers and reports Sales tax.</p><p>The relationship it solves reads Sales tax = get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code).</p><p>What comes back is Sales tax — each answer is a box on the form, so you read it beside the numbers that produced it.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What to enter, and what comes back', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Fill in 2 boxes and the page has everything it needs.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> — a plain number, no unit</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> — a plain number, no unit. For Ohio, ZIP codes are 5 numbers starting with a 4.</li></ul><p>What you get back is Sales tax, shown next to the form rather than on a page of its own.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Reading the answer', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>As far as Sales tax is concerned the numbers are unitless — it neither converts nor guesses a unit, so enter every value in the same scale you intend to read the answer in.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'The formula, and what turns it round', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>The relationship is shown here as the page solves it, with the form&#039;s labels standing where the variable names would.</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> = <code>get_sales_tax(Purchase amount, ZIP code)</code></li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Checking an answer by hand', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>There is one relationship on this page, so any answer can be checked by putting it back into the box that owns it and seeing whether the remaining ones agree.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Where the numbers usually go wrong', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <ul><li><strong>The answer typed back in.</strong> Sales tax is a result, and each can be typed into. Putting a past answer back into one of them turns the calculation round and solves for whichever box is then empty — useful, but only if that is what you meant to do.</li><li><strong>Purchase amount left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into Purchase amount is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>ZIP code left as a guess.</strong> Anything typed into ZIP code is treated as measured. The page has no way to flag an estimate, and the answer will read exactly as confidently either way.</li><li><strong>Measurements mixed across scales.</strong> Two numbers measured in different units, or one taken at the start of a season and one at the end, are the commonest source of an answer that is internally consistent and still wrong. Convert Purchase amount to one scale before entering anything.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each box means', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Before the arithmetic, the form itself. Each box below is named the way it is named on the page, with the unit it opens on and the error it invites if it is filled in carelessly.</p><ul><li><strong>Purchase amount</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. The page reads it when it works out Sales tax. Have it to hand before you start: Purchase amount is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li><li><strong>ZIP code</strong> takes a plain number with no unit attached. For Ohio, ZIP codes are 5 numbers starting with a 4. The page reads it when it works out Sales tax. Have it to hand before you start: ZIP code is read the moment another box is filled, and a placeholder typed to see what happens becomes an answer.</li></ul><p>None of the boxes has to be filled in a particular order. The page reads them together and works out which single value is missing, so the last one you fill is the one it answers from.</p>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'What each result tells you', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
            <p>Each box below is a different question about the same calculation. Read them in this order:</p><ul><li><strong>Sales tax</strong> is a plain number. The value follows from Purchase amount and ZIP code. Treat the first significant digit as the answer and the rest as information about precision — the last digits move when an input moves, and that is normal. It is not stored, logged or carried to the next page: change a box and you get a new number, close the tab and it is gone.</li></ul>
            TXT],
            ['title' => 'Getting an answer, step by step', 'html' => <<<'TXT'
